Well, you had better get off at the next stop with me. I think it's better to take a subway.
Excuse me, but does this bus go to Roppongi?
You missed your stop. Get off at the next stop and cross the street. You'll find the bus stop.
Take any bus going to Shibuya.
It doesn't take you more than ten minutes from there. Take care.
Hello. This is Suzuki.
Hello. Jim speaking.
Okay. I'll pick you up at your hotel at eight o'clock.
Okay. See you tomorrow. I'm looking forward to it. Good-bye.
I'm terribly sorry, but I can't visit you today. It seems I caught a cold.
Don't worry about it. We can make it some other time.
Excuse me. Where is a public telephone?
There is a pink one in front of the tea shop over there.
Can I call my friend in Osaka?
Are you calling Osaka? Then you ought to find a green or gray one, because your telephone card is available to it.
Where is the telephone book?
Put a ten yen coin in the slot first, then dial the number you want.
Do you know Mr. Tanaka's telephone number?
The area code of Tokyo is zero three.
This telephone is out of order.
The cherry blossoms are in full bloom now, aren't they? Did you go cherry blossom viewing anywhere?
Thank you. I'll be happy to come.
If you are not busy tomorrow, I'd like to invite you to dinner.
How about going to Yoko's birthday party with me?
I'm sorry, I have another engagement.
We call it sashimi or sliced raw fish.
Do you eat raw fish?
Yes. Most Japanese like it very much. We dip it in soy sauce with a little green horseradish. It's fresh and it doesn't taste fishy at all. Try it.
Is it true that Japanese like to eat poisonous fish?
The fish you mean is fugu. It has a deadly poison in its liver and ovaries.
Isn't it dangerous to eat it?
If it is prepared by a licensed cook, fugu is quite safe.
Have you ever seen Bunraku?
No, I haven't. But I'm eager to see it. That's the traditional Japanese puppet show, isn't it?
Yes. It's on at the National Theater now, and I've got two tickets. Would you like to go and see it with me on Sunday night?
The size of a puppet is about two-thirds the size of a real person. And each puppet is manipulated by three puppeteers.
Three puppeteers. Can you see the puppets well?
Yes, we can. The puppeteers wear black costumes so as not to distract the audience's attention from the puppets.
How big is Japan?
It's about three hundred eighty thousand square kilometers. That's about one hundred forty-six thousand square miles. What about the United States?
It's about three million six hundred ten thousand square miles. That's about twenty-five times the size of Japan.
The country of Japan consists of the four main islands and many other smaller islands.
The size of Japan is about one twenty-fifth the size of the United States.
Japan is as large as the size of state Montana.
Japan is in the Northern Hemisphere.
The Japanese islands run northeast to southwest in the northwestern part of the Pacific Ocean.
What's the population of Japan?
It's about one hundred twenty million.
One hundred twenty million people in a land of three hundred eighty thousand square kilometers?
Yes, that's right. The population density is about three hundred sixteen persons per square kilometer. A Japanese house is often called a rabbit hut. Maybe, the biggest reason why we are obliged to live in a small house is the high population density.
Japan's population is seventh in the world.
The population of Tokyo is about twelve million. It's crowded and the cost of housing is high.
So, most of the people working in Tokyo live in the suburbs.
This morning the trains and buses weren't crowded as usual. Why?
Because we're in the "O-Bon" season now.
It's one of the Buddhism festivals for the dead. Many people take a vacation and return to their home-towns and large cities appear empty.
Is O-Bon held in August every year?
Yes, it is. But in some parts of the country it is held in July.
The biggest holiday in Japan is the New Year holiday. For Japanese, the beginning of a new year is very important, and we all celebrate it. I think it is similar to Christmas in America.
According to the weather forecast, typhoon number ten is coming the Kanto area tomorrow morning.
Is it the typhoon season now?
Yes, it is. In Japan, the typhoon season lasts from late August to the beginning of October. Heavy rain and strong winds sometimes do great damage to our daily life. Farmers are especially afraid of them.
The rain and wind has stopped and the moon has appeared. Has the typhoon passed?
No, it hasn't. Now we are in the eye of the typhoon. Soon the heavy rain and strong winds will start again.
What fine weather we are having.
After the typhoon, we usually have a clear autumn day.
Yes, it is. We call the Japanese rainy season tsuyu.
How long does it last?
Usually it lasts from about mid-June to mid-July, but it doesn't necessarily rain everyday. Though it's an unpleasant season, it's necessary for rice planting.
Do you have the tsuyu in every area of Japan?
No, not everywhere. In Hokkaido, the weather is a little different from the other parts of Japan. They don't have tsuyu.
During tsuyu, it drizzles mostly. But sometimes it rains so heavy locally that it causes floods and landslides.
This is tofu or soybean curd. Have you ever tasted it?
No, I haven't. But tofu is popular as a health food in America, too. What is it made from?
Like miso and shoyu, it's made from soybeans. So, it's full of vegetable protein.
It seems good for the health.
How do you eat tofu?
In summer, "hiyayakko" is popular. We eat it with soy sauce, minced leek and grated ginger. We put it into misoshiru, too. And it is also one of the ingredients of sukiyaki. Anyway, tofu is a part of our daily life.
It's said that tofu was originated in China about two thousand years ago.
It's eleven o'clock now. Before going home let's eat some ramen at that stand. You'll find that after drinking, it tastes very good.
It smells nice. Is it a kind of soba?
Yes, it is. Ramen was originally a Chinese food. But it has already become firmly rooted among Japanese eating habits. It's good as a snack as well as for a meal.
No, it's free. In Japan, the delivery of dishes to the customer's house is popular, especially soba, sushi and ramen.
There are also many kinds of instant ramen. Cup type ramen can be prepared in three minutes after adding boiled water.
Which do you want, coffee, black tea or green tea?
I want green tea. In Japan, I got into the habit of drinking green tea after a meal.
Well, I'll make some with this year's freshest leaves.
That's nice.
Please enjoy its fresh smell with some yokan.
It's very sweet. Did you call it "yokan"?
Yes. Yokan is a typical Japanese sweet.
I wonder how they make it?
They mix red bean paste, sugar and Japanese gelatin, then knead or steam it.
I see. The taste of Japanese sweets is quite different from ours.
